{"reason": "The execution results confirm the overfitting pattern described in the initial plan: best validation log loss at epoch 2 in most folds, followed by degradation. The mean CV score of 0.3609 is close to the initial plan's expected improvement (0.345-0.355) but can be further optimized. Similar historical experiments show that using simpler classifiers (Logistic Regression) with TF-IDF and hand-crafted features can achieve competitive performance without overfitting, as seen in the successful example achieving stable log loss below 0.35. To stay true to the initial plan's direction (which focuses on training strategy modifications like gradient accumulation, learning rate schedule, and dropout) while incorporating insights from historical successes, I choose to modify 'training_evaluation' to implement the proposed changes (reduce gradient accumulation steps, use cosine annealing with warm restarts, increase dropout) and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to add TF-IDF and statistical features that can be concatenated as auxiliary inputs to the ModernBERT classifier, providing additional signals and improving generalization. This hybrid approach leverages the strengths of both deep learning and classical features, as validated by historical experiments.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add TF-IDF word and character n-gram features (up to 50k features) and statistical features (e.g., char/word counts, punctuation counts, archaic words) to be concatenated with ModernBERT embeddings. Compute these using sklearn's TfidfVectorizer on the clean text, scale numerical features with StandardScaler, and combine them into a dense feature vector. During training, extract these features for each fold without data leakage, and modify the model to accept these features as an additional input, concatenating them with the pooled output from ModernBERT before the classifier head. This enriches the input representation and reduces overfitting, as shown in successful historical experiments.", "training_evaluation": "Implement the three training strategy changes from the initial plan: 1) Reduce gradient accumulation steps from 2 to 1, making effective batch size 16, and increase learning rate to 3e-5. 2) Replace linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=2, T_mult=1) to periodically reset learning rate and escape sharp minima. 3) Increase dropout in classifier head from 0.3 to 0.5 and add dropout of 0.1 after GELU activation. Additionally, incorporate the hybrid model modification: take ModernBERT's pooled output, concatenate with extracted features, and pass through the higher-dropout classifier. This directly addresses overfitting (evidenced by validation loss increase after epoch 2) while leveraging feature engineering from similar successful experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the overfitting pattern described in the initial plan: best validation log loss at epoch 2 in most folds, followed by degradation. The mean CV score of 0.3609 is close to the initial plan's expected improvement (0.345-0.355) but can be further optimized. Similar historical experiments show that using simpler classifiers (Logistic Regression) with TF-IDF and hand-crafted features can achieve competitive performance without overfitting, as seen in the successful example achieving stable log loss below 0.35. To stay true to the initial plan's direction (which focuses on training strategy modifications like gradient accumulation, learning rate schedule, and dropout) while incorporating insights from historical successes, I choose to modify 'training_evaluation' to implement the proposed changes (reduce gradient accumulation steps, use cosine annealing with warm restarts, increase dropout) and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to add TF-IDF and statistical features that can be concatenated as auxiliary inputs to the ModernBERT classifier, providing additional signals and improving generalization. This hybrid approach leverages the strengths of both deep learning and classical features, as validated by historical experiments.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add TF-IDF word and character n-gram features (up to 50k features) and statistical features (e.g., char/word counts, punctuation counts, archaic words) to be concatenated with ModernBERT embeddings. Compute these using sklearn's TfidfVectorizer on the clean text, scale numerical features with StandardScaler, and combine them into a dense feature vector. During training, extract these features for each fold without data leakage, and modify the model to accept these features as an additional input, concatenating them with the pooled output from ModernBERT before the classifier head. This enriches the input representation and reduces overfitting, as shown in successful historical experiments.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement the three training strategy changes from the initial plan: 1) Reduce gradient accumulation steps from 2 to 1, making effective batch size 16, and increase learning rate to 3e-5. 2) Replace linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=2, T_mult=1) to periodically reset learning rate and escape sharp minima. 3) Increase dropout in classifier head from 0.3 to 0.5 and add dropout of 0.1 after GELU activation. Additionally, incorporate the hybrid model modification: take ModernBERT's pooled output, concatenate with extracted features, and pass through the higher-dropout classifier. This directly addresses overfitting (evidenced by validation loss increase after epoch 2) while leveraging feature engineering from similar successful experiments."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current model is severely underfitting (train loss ~0.79-0.86, val loss ~0.87-0.97) due to mixup augmentation corrupting semantic signal. The initial plan correctly identifies this root cause and proposes reverting to clean training. Similar historical experiments show that successful models use standard cross-entropy with label smoothing, differential learning rates, and cosine scheduling without mixup. Therefore, the most promising modules to modify are: 1) 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to simplify by removing TF-IDF features (as per initial plan), 2) 'model_design' to remove mixup from loss and return to standard cross-entropy with label smoothing, and 3) 'training_evaluation' to fix the DataLoader collate function, increase epochs, adjust learning rate, and remove SWA which is not needed without mixup. These changes directly address the core issue revealed in execution results and align with proven historical successes.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all TF-IDF and handcrafted feature engineering from the pipeline to let ModernBERT learn directly from raw text. This simplifies the code, reduces memory usage, and eliminates the risk of leakage from TF-IDF fitting per fold. The feature engineering functions (extract_lexical_features, extract_structural_features, engineer_features) and all TF-IDF vectorizers and scalers will be deleted. Data loaders will only pass tokenized text without any sparse features. This change aligns with the initial plan's observation that extra features are redundant for a 559M parameter model.", "model_design": "Remove the mixup loss (KLDivLoss) and return to standard nn.CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing of 0.1 (reduced from 0.2 to prevent over-smoothing). The model configuration (ModernBert-large) remains unchanged. The mixup_criterion variable and the soft label computation in the training loop will be removed. The loss computation will directly use the model's logits and the original labels. This follows successful historical experiments that used standard cross-entropy with label smoothing and achieved good convergence.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the mixup collate function with default_collate from PyTorch in the DataLoader. Increase NUM_EPOCHS to 6 and reduce LEARNING_RATE to 1e-5 with linear warmup over 10% of steps and cosine decay. Remove SWA (stochastic weight averaging) as it is not needed with clean training. Implement gradient clipping with max_norm=1.0 and keep gradient accumulation steps at 2. Increase early stopping patience to 3. These changes are informed by historical experiments that used differential learning rates and cosine scheduling without mixup, leading to lower validation loss."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model is severely underfitting (train loss ~0.79-0.86, val loss ~0.87-0.97) due to mixup augmentation corrupting semantic signal. The initial plan correctly identifies this root cause and proposes reverting to clean training. Similar historical experiments show that successful models use standard cross-entropy with label smoothing, differential learning rates, and cosine scheduling without mixup. Therefore, the most promising modules to modify are: 1) 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to simplify by removing TF-IDF features (as per initial plan), 2) 'model_design' to remove mixup from loss and return to standard cross-entropy with label smoothing, and 3) 'training_evaluation' to fix the DataLoader collate function, increase epochs, adjust learning rate, and remove SWA which is not needed without mixup. These changes directly address the core issue revealed in execution results and align with proven historical successes.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all TF-IDF and handcrafted feature engineering from the pipeline to let ModernBERT learn directly from raw text. This simplifies the code, reduces memory usage, and eliminates the risk of leakage from TF-IDF fitting per fold. The feature engineering functions (extract_lexical_features, extract_structural_features, engineer_features) and all TF-IDF vectorizers and scalers will be deleted. Data loaders will only pass tokenized text without any sparse features. This change aligns with the initial plan's observation that extra features are redundant for a 559M parameter model.",
    "model_design": "Remove the mixup loss (KLDivLoss) and return to standard nn.CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing of 0.1 (reduced from 0.2 to prevent over-smoothing). The model configuration (ModernBert-large) remains unchanged. The mixup_criterion variable and the soft label computation in the training loop will be removed. The loss computation will directly use the model's logits and the original labels. This follows successful historical experiments that used standard cross-entropy with label smoothing and achieved good convergence.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the mixup collate function with default_collate from PyTorch in the DataLoader. Increase NUM_EPOCHS to 6 and reduce LEARNING_RATE to 1e-5 with linear warmup over 10% of steps and cosine decay. Remove SWA (stochastic weight averaging) as it is not needed with clean training. Implement gradient clipping with max_norm=1.0 and keep gradient accumulation steps at 2. Increase early stopping patience to 3. These changes are informed by historical experiments that used differential learning rates and cosine scheduling without mixup, leading to lower validation loss."
  }
}"}

